            0.09,
            0.445,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, changing phases of the Moon is a natural periodic event, with a cycle of almost a month, which can also be used for time keeping."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.474,
            0.106,
            0.821,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Yes, along with the natural periodic events of day and night and the changing seasons about which we learnt earlier. But how are these periodic events used for keeping time?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.826,
            0.098,
            0.947,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.826,
                0.098,
                0.947,
                0.206
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.226,
            0.87,
            0.252
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.2 How Did Calendars Come into Existence?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.276,
            0.884,
            0.406
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have learnt earlier that when viewed from the Earth, the Sun appears to rise in the eastward direction, set in the westward direction every day, and rise again the next day. This apparent periodic motion of the Sun seen by us is primarily due to the rotation of the Earth around its own axis. This natural cycle of the Sun due to the rotation of the Earth, is the foundation of the day, a unit to measure time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.407,
            0.884,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The average time that the Sun takes to go from its highest position in the sky on one day to the highest position in the sky the next day, is 24 hours, and is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "mean solar day",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The highest position of the Sun in the sky can be found by measuring the length of the shadows cast by an object during the day. The shadow is shortest when the Sun is at the highest point in the sky."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.549,
            0.736,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 11.3: Let us measure a day!"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.59,
            0.883,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Find a small flat area in a ground which receives sunlight during the day. Fix a 1 m stick vertically in it as shown in Fig. 11.7."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.652,
            0.475,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.109,
                0.652,
                0.475,
                0.873
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.113,
                0.878,
                0.472,
                0.912
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 11.7: Observing the length of the shadow at different times during the day."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.652,
            0.883,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Start observing at 11:00 a.m. Every minute, mark a dot on the ground at the tip of the stick’s shadow. Keep marking dots until around 1:10 p.m."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.731,
            0.884,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Identify",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " when the shadow was shortest and find out its time by counting the number of dots. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Record",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " this time in Table 11.2. Repeat this exercise for the next few days."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.828,
            0.884,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Find the duration of the solar day by finding a difference in time on two consecutive days as shown in Table 11.2."
            }
          ]
        },
